WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.720
This here is a pile of keepsakes from
preschool.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.520
So what we have is just

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.910
a standard family portrait, some smiling
cat, masterfully drawn rainbows and

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.950
flowers, and then there's this. It's my
name written completely backward.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.760
Weird right?

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.700
Except I used to do this all the time.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.769
If it wasn't my full name it was usually
just a random letter or number, like I

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:27.960
did with the 'G' in kindergarten.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.580
It's called mirror writing, and most kids

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.180
under the age of 7 have done it at some
point, in some form.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.460
It seems kind of ridiculous now, but

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.750
it's completely normal.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.620
It's just a sign of how our brains
evolved to see the world.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.920
Learning to write is kind of hard.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.800
First, we have to understand a string of abstract shapes,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.920
then, as we learn, we have to understand
that, in English,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.260
script goes from left to right. Early on, before really understanding which direction to write in,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.480
kids like me just wrote where there
was empty space available.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.240
So if I put a 'K' on the right edge of the page,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.140
I just wrote the rest of my name into 
the empty space on the left.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.520
I would be willing to bet that you
started with the letter K,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.160
because you know that "K" is the
first letter of your name

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.460
and they progressed in the right-to-left
direction – writing the K backwards,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.000
the I and the M would also be written
backwards but we don't see that

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.720
because they look exactly the same.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.770
Rob McIntosh has studied various forms

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.360
of mirror writing for years.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.420
Much more common, actually, is
just partial mirror writing.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.060
Where the script might go in
the correct direction

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.480
but individual letters will be reversed.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.940
So maybe the letter "D" is written as a "B" or "B" is written as a "D."

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.600
You might be thinking, "So what? Kids are still learning

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.180
and just don't understand
the shapes yet."

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.820
But it's not likely for a kid to take that same letter and flip it completely upside down.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.869
And we're not talking about them confusing one letter for another entirely.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:57.100
Remember, I didn't use the wrong shapes.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.040
I used the right ones.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.070
In the right order –

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.720
just backward.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.720
So how did I manage to do that?

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.420
The short answer is that children can't
tell these mirror images apart at this stage.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.080
You know, we tend to think that it's
children are bad at discriminating the

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.200
mirror images, but we might turn that
around and say that actually that

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.360
children are very good at generalizing
across mirror image forms.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.067
And it seems to be that that's something the human brain is set up do.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:28.840
There's a name for this inability to tell mirror images apart: "mirror generalization"

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.500
and our adult minds do it all the time too.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.060
Think of the Statue of Liberty

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.520
You can probably picture that it's a
green statue that holds a torch but

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.690
What hand is the torch in?

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.800
I'm trying to think ...if I picture...

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.980
Oh, God.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.500
Oh I have no idea.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.560
I mean 50/50 guess I'm gonna say the right hand.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.280
Left?

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.600
Torch is in the right hand.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.120
Left.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.080
Are you sure?

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.090
No. [Laughs]

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.680
And you can probably describe the Apple logo ...

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.740
a silhouette of an apple with a bite mark out of it ...

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:04.240
but –

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.080
Which side is the bite taken out of?

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.960
Oh my god. I look at this every single
day.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.260
If I'm looking at it?

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:11.860
Yeah.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.260
it's on the left side of the Apple.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.440
I want to say the
right side.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.660
Uh, the left side.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:17.960
The left side.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.840
The trouble is that orientation as in
the left or right direction something is

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.340
facing is rarely committed to memory –

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.380
because in the natural world it doesn't really matter.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.040
When we see it an animal
facing to the left,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.740
we'll still recognize it as a dog when it turns around.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.460
When I'm sitting to the right of someone,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.960
I'm not suddenly shocked when they turn to face me or move to my left.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:44.020
Most objects in nature don't change their identity depending on which way round they're facing.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.780
Trees, plants, and animals basically look the same one way or another.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.116
So our brains cut corners to allow us to recognize these things quickly

00:03:51.116 --> 00:03:54.540
and commit them to memory without focusing on direction.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.580
So when it comes to letters and numbers and other things that depend on orientation

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.340
we have to work a little harder to remember
which way is right.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.080
We have to sort of suppress
the tendency to automatically generalize.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.280
Which is actually an amazing feat.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.520
Our brains have evolved to mirror generalize –

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.020
and we've taught them when and
how to turn that skill off.

